<DocPage eyebrow="GUIDE / GAME INPUT" title="Expose game actions and keyboard or pointer input" lead="Semantic UI operates visible controls such as buttons and input fields. Game input instead drives gameplay actions such as jump and move, or keyboard, pointer, and gamepad input. This lets an AI agent or test reproduce character and camera control outside the UI." description="Semantic Game Action and Raw Input capabilities, leases, confirmation, Godot, Unity, MCP, WebMCP, and Inspector." section="game-input">
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Enable Raw Input only when needed"><p>Raw Input can operate the keyboard and pointer directly. Enable it only when the game can receive those inputs correctly and always release held keys or buttons. It is separate from the Semantic UI <code>press_key</code> action that sends a key to an on-screen control.</p></Callout>
  <h2>How this differs from Semantic UI input</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Semantic UI</strong><p>Operate an on-screen control by ID or role, such as pressing the Start button or entering a name in a field.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Game input</strong><p>Drive gameplay itself, such as jumping, moving right, holding W, or moving the pointer.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Expose only the operations you need</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Semantic Game Action</strong><p>Publish game-defined actions such as <code>jump</code>, <code>move</code>, or <code>chat</code>. An agent can request the intent “jump” without knowing the player's key binding.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Raw Input</strong><p>Expose keyboard, pointer, Standard Gamepad, and text input close to the physical device model. Enable it only when testing key bindings or hold behavior.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Release held input safely</h2><p>If an agent stops while holding W, Gua prevents the character from walking forever. Held input is tracked separately for each connection, so one connection does not release another connection's controls. If no release command arrives, a hold ends automatically after five seconds by default and can never exceed 60 seconds. Disconnect, session disposal, reset, adapter shutdown, and replay failure also return keys and buttons to neutral. An operation is reported as successful only after the game host confirms that it processed the input.</p>
  <h2>Engine setup</h2><div class="grid-2"><div class="mini-card"><strong>Unity 6000.5+</strong><p>Add <code>GuaGameInputMap</code>, register only intended actions, and enable raw input only when the Input System pump is ready. Unity uses virtual Keyboard, Mouse, and Standard Gamepad devices from <code>com.unity.inputsystem@1.20.0</code>.</p></div><div class="mini-card"><strong>Godot 4.7</strong><p>Configure the action map on <code>GuaAutoAdapter</code>. Raw requests are injected on the main thread through <code>Input.parse_input_event</code>.</p></div></div>
  <h2>Tools and confirmation</h2><p><code>gui-mcp</code> advertises a fixed game-input tool surface and rejects an unsupported operation when it is invoked. WebMCP registers only the input tools enabled by the capabilities read during page-tool registration. The Inspector's direct panel provides Semantic Game Action controls, one-shot physical-key presses, held-state inspection, and <strong>Release all</strong>. Release all is a cleanup operation that releases every held key, button, movement action, or other game input owned by the current Inspector connection and returns the character or device state to neutral. It does not release input owned by another connection. Pointer, gamepad, and text input are not direct Inspector controls. A risky action marked <code>requiresConfirmation</code> must receive <code>confirmed: true</code>. Player/Public Agent input remains denied unless the host separately enables Player semantic or raw capabilities.</p>
  <Callout kind="tip" title="Sensitive input"><p>Mark secret text as sensitive. The value still reaches the host input path, but logs, diagnostics, errors, and recordings retain only redacted metadata or a replay secret reference.</p></Callout>
</DocPage>
